  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why can't a mantis shrimp punch with muscles alone?",
      "a": "Muscles are strong but slow to contract. By loading a spring slowly and releasing it all at once, the shrimp gets far more speed than any muscle could give, like a bow and arrow.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a spring make a punch faster?",
        "What powers a mantis shrimp punch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the saddle spring?",
      "a": "It is a curved, saddle-shaped piece of hard shell in the mantis shrimp's limb. It bends as the muscle pulls, storing energy like a bent bow, and springs back when the latch releases.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does a mantis shrimp store energy?",
        "How does the latch work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast is a mantis shrimp punch?",
      "a": "The peacock mantis shrimp's club reaches over 20 metres per second, with an acceleration over 10,000 times gravity, one of the fastest strikes of any animal. It can crack aquarium glass.",
      "asks": [
        "Can a mantis shrimp break glass?",
        "How strong is a mantis shrimp punch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is cavitation?",
      "a": "When something moves very fast through water, the pressure behind it drops so low that the water briefly forms vapour bubbles. When the bubbles collapse they release a burst of energy, heat and even a flash of light.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the mantis shrimp make bubbles?",
        "How does the shrimp hit twice?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why doesn't the club break?",
      "a": "The club is built in layers, with fibres stacked in a twisting spiral pattern that stops cracks spreading. Engineers study it to design tougher materials.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a mantis shrimp club made of?",
        "How does the club survive the impact?"
      ]
    }
